Title: The governance guard's scanned set is hand-listed, so a new spec document is unguarded by default
Created: 2026-08-29
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-08-29T13:13Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

`bench/check_governance.py` (ticket 0053, merged as PR #41) enforces the
governance/specification split by reading a hand-listed `SCANNED` set:

    SCANNED = ["README.md", "CLAUDE.md",
               "spec/REQUIREMENTS.md", "spec/CONSTRAINTS.md", "spec/DESIGN.md"]

The guard fails loudly when a listed document *vanishes*, which is the trap it
was written against. It says nothing at all when a document *appears*. So a new
specification document is unscanned by default, and its unscannedness is
indistinguishable from having nothing to hide.

This is 0161's shape exactly, one guard over: **the check validates what someone
declared, so a document nobody declared looks like a document with nothing to
declare.** 0161 records three independent instances of it in the figure guard,
and CLAUDE.md already names the family as this repo's most expensive recurring
defect. Filing it separately because the remedy differs — 0161's narrowing is by
provenance against `bench/results/**`, which has no analogue here.

The trap is live, not hypothetical: ticket 0051 adds `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md`, a
new document in the scanned directory. Nothing in the tree or in 0051 would
prompt whoever writes it to add a line to `SCANNED`, and `make check` stays green
either way.

The stake is not cosmetic. This guard is the one the author asked for so the
disclosure hazard would be "enforced by a check, not by care" (0053's
verification criterion) on a public repository the upstream maintainer reads.
A guard that silently stops covering new documents has reverted to care while
still reporting green.

## Relevant files
- `bench/check_governance.py` — `SCANNED`, and the `UNSCANNED_BY_DESIGN` list
  that currently documents the exclusions without enforcing them
- `tests/test_check_governance.py` — `build()` already constructs fixture repos
  from `cg.SCANNED`, so it inherits the same blind spot
- `tickets/0161-standing-guard-every-prose-doc-quoting-a.erg` — the same shape
  in the figure guard; read its "why a grep is not the answer" section first
- `tickets/0051-terminology-md-a-standalone-glossary-for.erg` — the document
  that will walk into this

## Actions
1. Invert the default: discover the candidate set from the tree (the `.md`
   files at the repository root plus `spec/`), and scan everything not named in
   an explicit exclusion list.
2. Promote `UNSCANNED_BY_DESIGN` from a comment into that enforced list, each
   entry keeping the reason it carries today.
3. Fail when a discovered document is in neither set — the point is that adding
   a document forces a decision instead of defaulting to silence.

## Test
Red first: a fixture repository containing `spec/TERMINOLOGY.md`, which states a
bound and appears in neither list. It must fail before the change and pass after
the document is classified. Assert the failure names the document.

A second test must confirm the exclusion list still excuses `spec/DECISIONS.md`
and `SYNC.md` — the append-only ledger and the live record legitimately state
bounds, and a fix that starts flagging them would be reverted within a day.

## Verification
- [ ] A new `.md` under `spec/` is scanned without anyone editing the guard
- [ ] A document in neither list fails the build and is named in the message
- [ ] `spec/DECISIONS.md` and `SYNC.md` stay excused, with their reasons in code
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants
- The guard keeps discriminating bounds from the actor: naming the maintainer
  is not a finding, and `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md`'s measured asymmetry must survive
  (0053 settled this; a fix that re-fires on "maintainer" is wrong).
- No ratified `spec/DECISIONS.md` entry is edited.
- Sabotage-check each new test, as `tests/test_check_figures.py` and
  `tests/test_check_governance.py` both do.

## Exit criteria
- Adding a specification document forces a scan/exclude decision rather than
  defaulting to unscanned, and the failure names the undeclared document.
